WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.046 --> 00:00:05.655
FAYE MOSS: Hello, everyone! Welcome back to Superstars

2
00:00:05.656 --> 00:00:18.783
in History . I'm your host, Faye Moss, and today's guest was a brilliant researcher who won not one but two Nobel Prizes for her work in physics and

3
00:00:18.784 --> 00:00:24.487
chemistry. She's also the first woman ever to win a Nobel

4
00:00:24.488 --> 00:00:47.311
Prize. Let's welcome a true science superstar, Marie

5
00:00:47.312 --> 00:00:48.842
Curie! MARIE CURIE: Thank

6
00:00:48.843 --> 00:00:55.905
you, Faye. I must confess I'd rather be back in a science lab, but I appreciate this opportunity to

7
00:00:55.906 --> 00:01:01.447
visit you! FAYE MOSS: We're excited to learn more about your groundbreaking

8
00:01:01.448 --> 00:01:04.754
research, Dr. Curie. But first let's talk

9
00:01:04.755 --> 00:01:12.220
about your childhood. You were born in 1867 in Warsaw, Poland, and named Maria

10
00:01:12.221 --> 00:01:16.446
Sklodowska, right? MARIE CURIE: Yes, and I was always

11
00:01:16.447 --> 00:01:25.460
a very serious student. My father, who was an educator, immersed our family in learning, teaching us several languages as well as

12
00:01:25.461 --> 00:01:33.892
geography and poetry. Because Father taught science, there was also scientific equipment around our home, and I learned how to use it at an

13
00:01:33.893 --> 00:01:39.269
early age. Oh, and I graduated high school at age 15, first in my

14
00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:44.592
class. FAYE MOSS: Whoa! I'm not surprised you were

15
00:01:44.593 --> 00:01:53.518
a spectacular student. But as a female growing up in the nineteenth century, you encountered at least a few obstacles on your

16
00:01:53.519 --> 00:02:01.964
career path. MARIE CURIE: Indeed! I desperately wanted to attend university, but educational opportunities in

17
00:02:01.965 --> 00:02:05.821
Poland were limited. Women weren't allowed to

18
00:02:05.822 --> 00:02:13.637
enroll at universities. Poland was occupied by Russia at the time, and the Russian government also made it difficult for many Poles to get

19
00:02:13.638 --> 00:02:19.816
an education. So my sister Bronya and I joined a secret organization called the Flying

20
00:02:19.817 --> 00:02:29.067
University, which met every night in different locations to educate women in anatomy, natural history, and yes, Polish

21
00:02:29.068 --> 00:02:34.551
culture. The sessions were interesting, but they weren't enough for me or Bronya.

22
00:02:34.552 --> 00:02:43.989
Eventually the two of us hatched a plan—we'd leave Poland and go to the Sorbonne, a university in Paris. FAYE

23
00:02:43.990 --> 00:02:47.736
MOSS: Great idea! Did you go to school

24
00:02:47.737 --> 00:02:53.494
together? MARIE CURIE: No, we couldn't afford that, so we decided that Bronya would

25
00:02:53.495 --> 00:03:00.550
go first. Meanwhile I stayed in Poland and worked as a governess, sending her money to help fund her

26
00:03:00.551 --> 00:03:10.502
education. In 1891 it was finally my turn, and I enrolled at the Sorbonne as "Marie," the French version of my

27
00:03:10.503 --> 00:03:16.958
name. FAYE MOSS: At the Sorbonne, you earned two degrees—one in physics and one

28
00:03:16.959 --> 00:03:28.212
in math. MARIE CURIE: That's correct. And even though I was very poor as a student, it was one of the most perfect times of my life—a new world had

29
00:03:28.213 --> 00:03:32.658
opened to me. Yet even in Paris, women

30
00:03:32.659 --> 00:03:37.449
experienced discrimination. Females couldn't vote and few

31
00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:40.858
attended university. Even fewer conducted

32
00:03:40.859 --> 00:03:45.674
scientific research. In fact, no female in France had ever earned a

33
00:03:45.675 --> 00:03:56.632
doctorate. FAYE MOSS: Speaking of research, you eventually acquired a famous lab partner . . . your husband, Pierre Curie! How did you

34
00:03:56.633 --> 00:04:04.042
two meet? MARIE CURIE: In 1894 I had a job studying the magnetic properties of steel

35
00:04:04.043 --> 00:04:11.521
and needed lab space. Friends introduced me to Pierre Curie, another physicist, thinking he might be able to find

36
00:04:11.522 --> 00:04:19.836
me a space. Shortly afterward we fell in love and married in 1895. It was a simple ceremony,

37
00:04:19.837 --> 00:04:25.910
of course. I wore a navy blue suit—a practical outfit that I could later wear to the

38
00:04:25.911 --> 00:04:31.687
lab. FAYE MOSS: Your research led to some important prizes, which

39
00:04:31.688 --> 00:04:35.858
we'll definitely discuss. For now, can you tell us more about

40
00:04:35.859 --> 00:04:46.521
your research? MARIE CURIE: I had become interested in the work of a French scientist, Henri Becquerel, who'd discovered that rocks containing uranium emit

41
00:04:46.522 --> 00:04:53.769
light or rays. I wanted to explore whether other chemical bodies had this property, which

42
00:04:53.770 --> 00:05:02.168
I called radioactivity. Using a device that measured electrical changes in the air, I found that the element thorium also

43
00:05:02.169 --> 00:05:04.203
emitted rays. FAYE MOSS:

44
00:05:04.204 --> 00:05:07.335
Did you find others? MARIE CURIE: Oh

45
00:05:07.336 --> 00:05:15.314
yes, and Pierre joined me. We discovered two new elements: polonium, which I named after my

46
00:05:15.315 --> 00:05:20.209
homeland of Poland, and radium. To our surprise, radium was even

47
00:05:20.210 --> 00:05:26.168
more radioactive than uranium. When we isolated some larger pieces of it, we

48
00:05:26.169 --> 00:05:30.334
saw that they glowed! We did this work in an old shed

49
00:05:30.335 --> 00:05:34.331
with broken windows. We burned our fingers and

50
00:05:34.332 --> 00:05:40.809
suffered from exhaustion. FAYE MOSS: Luckily you were able to publish papers about your research and earn

51
00:05:40.810 --> 00:05:46.249
a doctorate in 1903. You were also awarded the Nobel Prize

52
00:05:46.250 --> 00:05:50.653
in Physics that year! MARIE CURIE: I shared the prize with

53
00:05:50.654 --> 00:05:58.322
Henri Becquerel and my husband. Many people assumed that the two men had done most of the work, but my husband was sure to

54
00:05:58.323 --> 00:06:04.640
give me proper credit! Sadly, just three years later, Pierre died when he was struck by

55
00:06:04.641 --> 00:06:09.411
a horse-drawn wagon. I was devastated but had

56
00:06:09.412 --> 00:06:13.920
to carry on. We had two young daughters, and there was more work waiting in

57
00:06:13.921 --> 00:06:21.506
the lab. When the physics department at the Sorbonne offered me Pierre's job, I became the school's first

58
00:06:21.507 --> 00:06:25.360
female professor. FAYE MOSS: You certainly did carry

59
00:06:25.361 --> 00:06:32.301
on, Dr. Curie! In 1911 you were awarded a second Nobel Prize, this

60
00:06:32.302 --> 00:06:38.386
time in chemistry. Then World War I broke out. That interrupted your

61
00:06:38.387 --> 00:06:44.494
research, right? MARIE CURIE: By 1914 I had helped to establish the Radium

62
00:06:44.495 --> 00:06:49.283
Institute, a research center. But before I could begin working there,

63
00:06:49.284 --> 00:06:54.552
the war arrived. I soon found a new way to use my scientific

64
00:06:54.553 --> 00:07:04.590
background—and save lives. X-rays, which had been discovered in 1895, were being used to diagnose patients' injuries, but this equipment was only available at

65
00:07:04.591 --> 00:07:14.885
city hospitals. So I worked with the Red Cross to set up twenty cars with x-ray machines—"little Curies"—that could be driven right to the battlefield.

66
00:07:14.886 --> 00:07:20.034
I also set up two hundred field hospitals with radiography rooms. At

67
00:07:20.035 --> 00:07:27.589
first I didn't know how to use the x-ray technology, but I taught myself and trained other women to use the machines too. FAYE MOSS:

68
00:07:27.590 --> 00:07:32.953
Thanks to you, over a million soldiers were x-rayed during the war.

69
00:07:32.954 --> 00:07:36.052
What did you do when the fighting was over? MARIE

70
00:07:36.053 --> 00:07:43.679
CURIE: I went back to the Radium Institute, and later began touring the world and giving lectures to raise funds for the institute.

71
00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:49.097
But I was impatient with all the travel and longed to be back in my quiet lab. And

72
00:07:49.098 --> 00:07:57.213
by 1933 I'm afraid I often felt ill, probably due to years of working with radiation. FAYE

73
00:07:57.214 --> 00:08:03.805
MOSS: We certainly don't want to wear you out here, Dr. Curie, so we'll let you return to history.

74
00:08:03.806 --> 00:08:08.067
Thank you for your contributions to science and medicine! MARIE

75
00:08:08.068 --> 00:08:14.233
CURIE: I appreciate that, Faye.

76
00:08:14.234 --> 00:08:24.441
Good-bye. FAYE MOSS: In 1934 Marie Curie's health was deteriorating, and she died that same year at the age

77
00:08:24.442 --> 00:08:35.139
of 66. The cause of her death was long-term exposure to radioactive material, which likely came from her work with x-ray equipment during World War

78
00:08:35.140 --> 00:08:42.680
I. A pioneer in physics and chemistry, she paved the way for future female

79
00:08:42.681 --> 00:08:53.303
scientists. Her research also led to many technologies in energy, space, and medicine, especially the use of radiation to treat cancer.

80
00:08:53.304 --> 00:08:57.384
I hope you enjoyed my interview with Marie Curie. You've

81
00:08:57.385 --> 00:09:07.082
been watching Faye Moss on Superstars in History !

